<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="84037e9a">&lt;p&gt;
    A number of frameworks and views have been defined for capturing architecture decisions and modeling the relationships.
    The details captured for each decision should support the subsequent activities, such as analysis of options and
    communication of final decisions to stakeholders.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="externalId" nodeId="c3e350d3"></attribute>
<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="76669030"></attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="57be8630"></attribute>
<attribute name="purpose" nodeId="e809b86b">To facilitate communication of rationale to stakeholders, support architectural reviews and evaluations and enable
reusability of architecture decisions.</attribute>
<attribute name="impactOfNotHaving" nodeId="1ba67644"></attribute>
<attribute name="reasonsForNotNeeding" nodeId="78e8f741"></attribute>
<attribute name="briefOutline" nodeId="3cec22de">&lt;p&gt;
    Architecture decisions usually include &lt;sup&gt;[1]&lt;/sup&gt;:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Short title&lt;/strong&gt; - provide a short title for the decision for ease of reference.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Architecture issue&lt;/strong&gt; - describe the architectural design issue being addressed to avoid
    misunderstandings about why the issue is being addressed at the given time. Following a minimalist approach, address,
    and document only the issues to be addressed at various points within the lifecycle.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Descriptions&lt;/strong&gt; - clearly state the direction for the architecture and the position selected.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Status&lt;/strong&gt; – the current state or position of the decision at a particular time (e.g., pending, decided,
    approved).
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Domain and group&lt;/strong&gt; - use a simple grouping, such as integration, presentation, data, to help
    organize the set of decisions.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Assumptions&lt;/strong&gt; - clearly describe the underlying assumptions within the environment for the decision
    (e.g., cost, schedule, technology). Note environmental constraints, for example, accepted technology standards,
    enterprise architecture, or commonly employed patterns, may limit the alternatives considered.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Constraints&lt;/strong&gt; - capture any additional environmental constraints the chosen alternative and decision may
    pose.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Options&lt;/strong&gt; - list the alternatives considered. These often require long explanations, sometimes even
    models and diagrams.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt; - outline why a particular position was selected, include implementation cost, total
    ownership cost, time to market and the required development resources’ availability. This is probably as important as
    the decision itself.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Implications&lt;/strong&gt; - a decision comes with many implications. For example, a decision may introduce a need
    to make other decisions, create new requirements or modify existing requirements, pose additional constraints to the
    environment, require renegotiating the scope or schedule with customers, or require additional staff training. Clearly
    understanding and stating the implications of a decision can be very effective in gaining buy-in and creating a roadmap
    for architecture execution.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Related decisions&lt;/strong&gt; – lists similar associated decisions. A traceability matrix, decision tree or
    meta-model may be more useful. Meta-models show complex relationships diagrammatically.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Related requirements&lt;/strong&gt; - decisions should be business-driven. To show accountability, explicitly map
    decisions to the objectives or requirements, or reference a traceability matrix for convenience purposes. Assess the
    contribution of each architecture decision to meeting each requirement, and then assess how well the requirement is met
    across all decisions. If a decision does not contribute to meeting a requirement, do not make that decision.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Related artifacts&lt;/strong&gt; - list the related architecture, design or scope documents the decision impacts.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Related principles&lt;/strong&gt; - if the enterprise has an agreed-upon set of principles, confirm the decision is
    consistent with one or more of the principles to help align with the domains or systems.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Additional information&lt;/strong&gt; - capture notes and issues the team discusses during the socialization process
    for ease of reference since the decision-making process can take weeks.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;sup&gt;[1]&lt;/sup&gt; Tyree, J., Akerman, A. Architecture decisions: demystifying architecture. IEEE Software 22 (2),
    2005 19–27
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
